pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Raise an exception with a message when a call cannot be served, and read the traceback of one that nobody handles"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can raise an exception with a message when a call cannot be served, and read the traceback of one that nobody handles, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter5.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO6> .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Choose between raise and assert for a given error, and explain why an assert cannot guard user input"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can choose between raise and assert for a given error, and explain why an assert cannot guard user input, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter5.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO6> .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Set up logging with a level and a format, and predict which messages a given threshold shows"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can set up logging with a level and a format, and predict which messages a given threshold shows, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter5.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO6> .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Find a bug by tracing it with log messages, and repair it"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can find a bug by tracing it with log messages, and repair it, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter5.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO6> .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Use breakpoints and the Continue, Step In, Step Over and Step Out controls to inspect a program's state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can use breakpoints and the Continue, Step In, Step Over and Step Out controls to inspect a program's state, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter5.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO6> .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The function check(age) raises ValueError('age cannot be negative') when age is below 0, and otherwise returns age. What is the last line Python prints for check(-3) when nobody handles the exception?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The traceback ends with the exception's type and message, not the function's name and argument." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The raise statement stops check before the return statement." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "ValueError: age cannot be negative" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "check: -3" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "-3" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A person types abc when the program asks for their age. Which instrument fits?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Under python -O the assert would not run, and this is an error of the user, not of the programmer." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A log message records the event; it does not decide what the program does about it." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "assert age.isdigit()" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "if / raise, and handle the exception" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "logging.debug('bad age')" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Under python -O, what happens to the statement  assert False, 'boom'?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Optimisation removes assert statements, so nothing is raised." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The syntax is valid; the statement is simply not compiled." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raises AssertionError as usual" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It is removed, so nothing happens" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It is a SyntaxError" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A program never calls basicConfig. It runs logging.debug('a'), logging.info('b'), logging.warning('c'). What appears?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The default threshold is WARNING, so the debug and info calls are dropped." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The warning call is at the threshold, so it is shown, on standard error." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "a, b and c" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "only c" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "nothing" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What is the number of the level logging.ERROR?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Remember ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "30 is WARNING." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "50 is CRITICAL." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "40" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "30" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "50" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The program calls logging.disable(logging.CRITICAL) and then logging.critical('x'). What happens?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "disable(CRITICAL) silences CRITICAL and everything below it." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Disabled messages are dropped without an error." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "x is shown" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Nothing is shown" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "logging raises an exception" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "basicConfig(level=logging.INFO) is called, then basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG), then logging.debug('x'). What is shown?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The second call changed nothing, so the threshold is still INFO." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "basicConfig does not complain; unless force=True is passed it simply does nothing." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "x" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Nothing: the second call is ignored" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "An exception" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "With the level at INFO, log.debug('v %s', obj) and log.debug(f'v {obj}') are called. When is obj converted to text?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The call with an argument formats only when the message is emitted." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The f-string builds its text before the call, whether or not the message is shown." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "In both calls" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Only in the f-string call" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "In neither call" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Which control runs a called function at full speed without stopping inside it?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Step In goes inside the call." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Step Out finishes the current function; it does not skip a call on the next line." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Step In" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Step Over" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Step Out" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The debugger is stopped on a line inside add, and you want to be back in the caller. Which control?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Continue runs to the next breakpoint, which may be far away." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Step In would go deeper, not back." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Continue" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Step In" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Step Out" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "factorial(3) loops over range(n + 1) and multiplies total, starting at 1, by i. What does it return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "6 needs the loop to start at 1; range(n + 1) starts at 0." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Multiplying by 0 is not an error; it makes total 0 on the first pass." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "6" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "0" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "An error" .
pg:BugModel a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Bugs and debugging"@en ;
    skos:definition "A bug is a place where a program does something other than what its author meant it to do, and debugging is the work of finding and removing such places. Chapter 5 opens with the observation that once a learner can write basic programs, the bugs become less simple, and it offers tools and techniques for finding the root cause of a bug quickly and with little effort (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_BugModel> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BugModel" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BugModel .
pg:BugNature a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Nature of a bug"@en ;
    skos:definition "The nature of a bug concerns what kind of thing a bug is and how it shows itself. Python distinguishes errors that stop a program before it starts, errors that stop it while it runs, and mistakes that do not stop it at all."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_BugNature> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BugNature" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BugModel .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BugNature .
pg:Bug a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Bug"@en ;
    skos:definition "A bug is a defect in a program: a line, a missing line or a wrong assumption that makes the program behave differently from what its author intended. The word describes the gap between the intention in the programmer's head and the behaviour of the running program, and not the computer, which carries out every instruction exactly as written."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Bug> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Bug" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BugNature .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Bug .
pg:LogicError a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logic error"@en ;
    skos:definition "A logic error is a mistake in the reasoning of a program that lets it run to the end without raising any exception and yet produces a wrong result. It is different from a syntax error, which the Python tutorial describes as a parsing error that stops the program from starting, and from an exception, which the tutorial defines as an error detected during execution (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LogicError> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogicError" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BugNature .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogicError .
pg:Debugging a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Debugging"@en ;
    skos:definition "Debugging is the activity of finding the root cause of a bug and removing it. The chapter introduces it as a set of tools and techniques that identify exactly what the code is doing and where it is going wrong (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Debugging> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Debugging" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BugNature .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Debugging .
pg:InstrumentSelection a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Instrument selection"@en ;
    skos:definition "Instrument selection is the choice of which of the chapter's four tools suits a given question. The chapter closes its summary by listing them together: assertions, exceptions, logging and the debugger are all valuable tools to find and prevent bugs (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_InstrumentSelection> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InstrumentSelection" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BugModel .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InstrumentSelection .
pg:InstrumentChoice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Choosing by question"@en ;
    skos:definition "Choosing an instrument means asking what the problem is and then picking the tool built for that kind of problem. The chapter's summary supplies the material: assertions are a good way to implement sanity checks that give early warning when a necessary condition does not hold, an exception can be caught and handled by try and except statements, the logging module looks into the code while it runs, and the debugger steps through the program one line at a time (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_InstrumentChoice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InstrumentChoice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstrumentSelection .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InstrumentChoice .
pg:WorkingVocabulary a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Working vocabulary"@en ;
    skos:definition "The working vocabulary gathers three everyday ideas that the chapter's tools take for granted: objects with their attributes and methods, the command line, and files with the directories that hold them. None of them is a tool for finding bugs."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_WorkingVocabulary> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WorkingVocabulary" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BugModel .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WorkingVocabulary .
pg:ObjectVocabulary a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Objects, attributes and methods"@en ;
    skos:definition "An object, in the words of the glossary, is any data with state, that is attributes or a value, and defined behavior, that is methods (Python Software Foundation, 2026). A class is a template for creating user-defined objects, and the objects made from it are its instances."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ObjectVocabulary> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ObjectVocabulary" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkingVocabulary .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ObjectVocabulary .
pg:CommandLine a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Command line"@en ;
    skos:definition "The command line is the line of text that a person types in a terminal to start a program. A terminal is the text window in which such lines are typed and their answers shown."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_CommandLine> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CommandLine" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkingVocabulary .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CommandLine .
pg:FileAndDirectory a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "File and directory"@en ;
    skos:definition "A file is a named piece of data stored on the computer, which a program reads or writes through a file object. The tutorial explains that open() returns a file object and takes the filename as its first argument, a string, and a mode as its second (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_FileAndDirectory> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FileAndDirectory" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkingVocabulary .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FileAndDirectory .
pg:ErrorSignalling a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Error signalling"@en ;
    skos:definition "Error signalling is the way a program says that something has gone wrong. In Python the saying is done with exceptions: an exception is an object that carries a description of the problem and travels up through the running code until something handles it."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ErrorSignalling> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ErrorSignalling" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ErrorSignalling .
pg:Raising a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Raising"@en ;
    skos:definition "Raising is the act of signalling an exception on purpose. The chapter's section Raising Exceptions explains that raising an exception is a way of saying: stop running this code, and move the program execution to the except statement (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Raising> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Raising" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ErrorSignalling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Raising .
pg:RaiseStatement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Raise statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "A raise statement signals an exception on purpose. In the chapter it consists of the keyword raise, a call to Exception, and a string with a helpful error message passed to that call (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_RaiseStatement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RaiseStatement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Raising .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RaiseStatement .
pg:ExceptionClass a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exception class"@en ;
    skos:definition "An exception class is the kind of an exception, and each kind is a class. A class is a pattern from which objects are made, and a subclass is a class that inherits from another and so counts as a kind of that other class."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ExceptionClass> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExceptionClass" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Raising .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExceptionClass .
pg:ExceptionMessage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exception message"@en ;
    skos:definition "The message of an exception is the text that was passed to it when it was created. The exception stores its arguments in the attribute args, which is a tuple, a fixed sequence of values, and calling str on the exception returns the arguments as text, or the empty string if there were none (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ExceptionMessage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExceptionMessage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Raising .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExceptionMessage .
pg:ArgumentValidation a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Argument validation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Argument validation is the practice of checking the values a function receives before the function uses them, and raising an exception when they cannot be used. The chapter's function box_print validates its three arguments with if statements, one for the length of the symbol and two for the size, and each failed test is followed by a raise statement with a message (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ArgumentValidation> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ArgumentValidation" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Raising .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ArgumentValidation .
pg:UnhandledException a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Unhandled exception"@en ;
    skos:definition "An unhandled exception is one that no except clause matches. The chapter describes the consequence in one sentence: if no try and except statements cover the raise statement that raised the exception, the program simply crashes and displays the exception's error message (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_UnhandledException> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UnhandledException" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Raising .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UnhandledException .
pg:Tracebacks a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tracebacks"@en ;
    skos:definition "A traceback is the report that Python prints when an exception is not handled. It lists the chain of function calls that led to the line where the exception was raised, ending with the class and the message of the exception."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Tracebacks> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Tracebacks" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ErrorSignalling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Tracebacks .
pg:ExceptionUnwinding a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exception unwinding"@en ;
    skos:definition "Exception unwinding is the way an exception travels from the place where it was raised back through the functions that were called to get there. The call stack is the list of functions that are currently running, the newest on top."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ExceptionUnwinding> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExceptionUnwinding" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tracebacks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExceptionUnwinding .
pg:TracebackReading a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Traceback reading"@en ;
    skos:definition "Reading a traceback means taking its lines in order and understanding what each one says. The first line is the header Traceback (most recent call last):."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_TracebackReading> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TracebackReading" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tracebacks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TracebackReading .
pg:FineGrainedLocations a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Fine grained locations"@en ;
    skos:definition "Fine grained locations are the marks that a traceback draws under a line of source to show which part of it failed. A row of carets, ^, marks the failing operation, and a row of tildes, ~, runs under the part of the expression that it was applied to."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_FineGrainedLocations> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FineGrainedLocations" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tracebacks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FineGrainedLocations .
pg:ExceptionNotes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Exception notes"@en ;
    skos:definition "An exception note is a short piece of text added to an exception after it has been created. The method add_note(note) adds a string to the exception's notes, which are kept in the list __notes__, and the standard traceback shows the notes after the exception string (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ExceptionNotes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ExceptionNotes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tracebacks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ExceptionNotes .
pg:StandardErrorStream a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Standard error stream"@en ;
    skos:definition "The standard error stream is a second channel of output that every program has next to its ordinary output. Python's documentation describes the standard streams as file objects: stdin is used for all interactive input, including calls to input; stdout is used for the output of print and for the prompts of input; and the interpreter's own prompts and error messages go to stderr (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_StandardErrorStream> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StandardErrorStream" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Tracebacks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StandardErrorStream .
pg:Assertion a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Assertion"@en ;
    skos:definition "An assertion is a statement that a certain condition must be true at a certain point of a program, together with a check that stops the program at once if it is not. The chapter calls an assertion a sanity check, a test that makes sure the code is not doing something obviously wrong, and it performs such checks with the assert statement, which raises an AssertionError exception when the check fails (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Assertion> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Assertion" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Assertion .
pg:Assert a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Assert"@en ;
    skos:definition "The assert group covers the mechanics and the discipline of assertions. The mechanics are the statement, the check it performs, the error it raises and the message it can carry."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Assert> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Assert" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assertion .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Assert .
pg:SanityCheck a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sanity check"@en ;
    skos:definition "A sanity check is a quick test that a result is not obviously wrong. It does not prove that a program is correct; it checks a property that must hold if the preceding code did its job, and it fails loudly when the property is violated."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_SanityCheck> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SanityCheck" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SanityCheck .
pg:AssertStatement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Assert statement"@en ;
    skos:definition "The assert statement checks a condition and raises an AssertionError exception if the condition is false. In the chapter it consists of the keyword assert, a condition that is an expression evaluating to True or False, and optionally a comma followed by a string to display when the condition is False (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_AssertStatement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AssertStatement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AssertStatement .
pg:SortedOrderCheck a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sorted-order check"@en ;
    skos:definition "The sorted-order check is the chapter's example of an assertion. It uses a list, which is an ordered collection of values written between square brackets, such as [26, 57, 92]."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_SortedOrderCheck> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SortedOrderCheck" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SortedOrderCheck .
pg:AssertionFailure a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Assertion failure"@en ;
    skos:definition "An assertion failure is the event in which an assert statement finds its condition false and raises AssertionError. The built-in exceptions page describes the class in one line: raised when an assert statement fails (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_AssertionFailure> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AssertionFailure" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AssertionFailure .
pg:AssertMessage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Assert message"@en ;
    skos:definition "The assert message is the optional second part of an assert statement. After the condition comes a comma and an expression, usually a string, and if the condition is false the value of that expression becomes the message of the AssertionError."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_AssertMessage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AssertMessage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AssertMessage .
pg:FailFast a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Fail fast"@en ;
    skos:definition "Failing fast is the principle of stopping a program at the first sign that something is wrong. The chapter uses the phrase for assertions: by failing fast like this, the time between the original cause of the bug and the moment of first noticing it is shortened, and so is the amount of code that has to be checked to find the cause (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_FailFast> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FailFast" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FailFast .
pg:AssertVersusRaise a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Assert versus raise"@en ;
    skos:definition "Assert and raise are two ways of stopping a program that has met a bad condition, and the chapter assigns each a different kind of error. Assertions are for programmer errors, not user errors."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_AssertVersusRaise> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AssertVersusRaise" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AssertVersusRaise .
pg:OptimisedMode a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Optimised mode"@en ;
    skos:definition "Optimised mode is a way of running Python in which assert statements are removed. The command-line option -O removes assert statements and any code that is conditional on the value of __debug__ (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_OptimisedMode> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OptimisedMode" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OptimisedMode .
pg:CommandLineOption a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Command-line option"@en ;
    skos:definition "A command-line option is a word that begins with a hyphen and is written after the name of the interpreter to change how it runs, as in python -O script.py. The documentation gives the general form python [-bBdEhiIOPqRsSuvVWx?] [-c command | -m module-name | script | - ] [args], and says that the most common use is simply python myscript.py (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_CommandLineOption> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CommandLineOption" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Assert .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CommandLineOption .
pg:Logging a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logging"@en ;
    skos:definition "Logging is the practice of making a program write down what it does while it runs. The Python logging HOWTO defines it as a means of tracking events that happen when some software runs: the developer adds logging calls to the code to indicate that certain events have occurred, and each event is described by a message and has an importance, which is called its level or severity (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Logging> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Logging" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Logging .
pg:Setup a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Setup"@en ;
    skos:definition "Setting up logging means telling the module three things: which messages count, how a message should look and where it should go. The chapter does all three with one call placed at the top of the program, logging.basicConfig, with the arguments level, format and, optionally, filename (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Setup> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Setup" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Logging .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Setup .
pg:BasicConfig a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Basic config"@en ;
    skos:definition "The function logging.basicConfig does the basic configuration of the logging system. According to the library reference it creates a StreamHandler with a default Formatter and adds it to the root logger, and its keyword arguments choose the level, the format string, a file name, a stream and a few other options (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_BasicConfig> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BasicConfig" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Setup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BasicConfig .
pg:LogFormat a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Log format"@en ;
    skos:definition "The log format is a string that describes the layout of each line a log call produces. It is a template in which named fields, written in the form %(name)s, stand for the parts of a log record."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LogFormat> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogFormat" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Setup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogFormat .
pg:PercentPlaceholder a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Percent placeholder"@en ;
    skos:definition "A percent placeholder is a marker such as %s or %d in a string that is replaced by a value. The Python reference calls the % operator on strings the string formatting or interpolation operator: given format % values, the conversion specifications in the format are replaced with elements of values, as in the example '%s has %d quote types.' % ('Python', 2), which gives 'Python has 2 quote types.' (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_PercentPlaceholder> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PercentPlaceholder" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Setup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PercentPlaceholder .
pg:LogToFile a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Log to file"@en ;
    skos:definition "Logging to a file means sending the log messages to a text file instead of the screen. A file is a named sequence of characters kept on disk, and a text file can be read in any text editor."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LogToFile> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogToFile" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Setup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogToFile .
pg:ForceReconfigure a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Force reconfigure"@en ;
    skos:definition "Force reconfigure is the option that makes basicConfig replace an existing configuration. The library reference states that the function does nothing if the root logger already has handlers configured, unless the keyword argument force is set to true, in which case the existing handlers are removed and closed before the new configuration is carried out (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ForceReconfigure> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ForceReconfigure" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Setup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ForceReconfigure .
pg:LoggerObjects a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Loggers and handlers"@en ;
    skos:definition "Behind the simple module-level functions of logging stands a small system of objects. The Python documentation says that the library takes a modular approach and offers several categories of components: loggers expose the interface that application code uses, handlers send the log records to the right destination, filters decide finer which records to output, and formatters specify the layout of the final output (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LoggerObjects> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoggerObjects" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Logging .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoggerObjects .
pg:Logger a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logger"@en ;
    skos:definition "A logger is the object on which log calls are made. Each logger has a name, and the names are arranged in a hierarchy by dots, so that the logger 'app.db' is a child of 'app', and all loggers are descendants of the root logger."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Logger> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Logger" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoggerObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Logger .
pg:Handler a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Handler"@en ;
    skos:definition "A handler is the object that delivers a log record to a destination. The logging documentation lists several: a StreamHandler writes to a stream and uses sys.stderr when none is given, a FileHandler writes to a disk file, and there are handlers for email and for system logs, among others (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Handler> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Handler" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoggerObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Handler .
pg:LogRecord a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Log record"@en ;
    skos:definition "A log record is the object that a logger creates for each event that is logged. It contains all the information pertinent to the event: the name of the logger, the numeric level, the path and line number of the call, the message and the arguments to merge into it (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LogRecord> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogRecord" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LoggerObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogRecord .
pg:Levels a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Levels"@en ;
    skos:definition "Levels are the importance ranks that log messages carry. There are five of them, from the least to the most important, and each has a name, a number and a function of the same name."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Levels> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Levels" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Logging .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Levels .
pg:LoggingLevels a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logging levels"@en ;
    skos:definition "The five logging levels are DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR and CRITICAL, in increasing order of importance. Each is a number in the logging module: 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50, and a sixth value, NOTSET, which is 0, means that no level is set (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LoggingLevels> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoggingLevels" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Levels .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoggingLevels .
pg:LevelThreshold a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Level threshold"@en ;
    skos:definition "The level threshold is the level below which messages are ignored. It is set with the level argument of basicConfig, and a logger set to a threshold accepts messages of that level and above."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LevelThreshold> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LevelThreshold" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Levels .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LevelThreshold .
pg:DefaultLevel a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Default level"@en ;
    skos:definition "The default level is the threshold that applies when the program has not configured logging. The documentation states that the default level is WARNING, which means that only events of this severity and higher are tracked unless the logging package is configured to do otherwise, and that the root logger is created with the level WARNING (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_DefaultLevel> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DefaultLevel" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Levels .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DefaultLevel .
pg:DisablingLogging a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Disabling logging"@en ;
    skos:definition "Disabling logging means switching log messages off from one place, without deleting the calls. The function logging.disable(level) provides an overriding level for all loggers, which takes precedence over each logger's own level, and its effect is to disable all logging calls of severity level and below (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_DisablingLogging> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DisablingLogging" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Levels .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DisablingLogging .
pg:Practice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "Practice in logging is the set of habits that decide whether log messages help or hinder. The first habit is to choose logging and not print for the diagnostic output of a program, and the second is to write the log calls so that they cost little when the messages are not wanted."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Practice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Practice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Logging .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Practice .
pg:LoggingVersusPrint a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logging versus print"@en ;
    skos:definition "Logging and print both write text, but they serve different readers. The chapter's rule is that log messages are intended for the programmer, not the user, and that for error messages the user should see, like File not found or Invalid input, please enter a number, a print call is the right tool (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LoggingVersusPrint> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoggingVersusPrint" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Practice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoggingVersusPrint .
pg:LazyFormatting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Lazy formatting"@en ;
    skos:definition "Lazy formatting means that the text of a log message is built only if the message is actually going to be emitted. A call such as log.debug('value is %s', obj) hands the message and the argument separately to the logger; the HOWTO says that formatting of message arguments is deferred until it cannot be avoided (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_LazyFormatting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LazyFormatting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Practice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LazyFormatting .
pg:Debugger a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Debugger"@en ;
    skos:definition "The debugger is a tool that runs a program one instruction at a time and lets the programmer look at the program between the steps. The chapter defines it as a feature of the Mu editor that executes a program one instruction at a time, giving a chance to inspect the values in variables while the code runs and to track how the values change over the course of the program (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Debugger> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Debugger" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Debugger .
pg:DebuggerInterface a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Debugger interface"@en ;
    skos:definition "The debugger interface is everything the person at the keyboard sees and touches while a program runs under the debugger. The chapter describes it for the Mu editor: the editor window in which the debugger is started, the Debug button that starts it, the Debug Inspector pane that lists the values of the variables, and the Stop button that ends the session (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_DebuggerInterface> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DebuggerInterface" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Debugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DebuggerInterface .
pg:MuEditor a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Mu editor"@en ;
    skos:definition "Mu is the code editor that the chapter uses for its programs. An editor is a program for writing source code, and Mu adds to it the means to run a program and to run it under the debugger."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_MuEditor> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MuEditor" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DebuggerInterface .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MuEditor .
pg:RunUnderDebugger a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Running under the debugger"@en ;
    skos:definition "Running a program under the debugger means starting it through the debugger instead of running it directly. The program does not run to its end at once."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_RunUnderDebugger> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RunUnderDebugger" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DebuggerInterface .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RunUnderDebugger .
pg:ProgramState a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Program state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The state of a program is what the program holds at one moment: the values of its variables and the position of the line that is about to run. The chapter describes the debugger as a way to inspect the values in variables while the code runs and to track how the values change over the course of the program (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ProgramState> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ProgramState" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DebuggerInterface .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ProgramState .
pg:InspectorPane a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Debug Inspector pane"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Debug Inspector pane is the part of the Mu window that lists the current value of each variable of the program. It opens at the right side of the window when the program is run under the debugger (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_InspectorPane> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InspectorPane" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DebuggerInterface .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InspectorPane .
pg:StopControl a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Stop button"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Stop button ends a debugging session immediately. The chapter says that to stop debugging entirely, without bothering to execute the rest of the program, one clicks the Stop button, and that it immediately terminates the program (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_StopControl> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StopControl" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DebuggerInterface .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StopControl .
pg:Controls a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Controls"@en ;
    skos:definition "The controls are the means by which the programmer moves a paused program forward, or tells it where to pause. The chapter lists the buttons that Debugging mode adds to the editor: Continue, Step Over, Step In and Step Out (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Controls> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Controls" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Debugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Controls .
pg:Breakpoint a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Breakpoint"@en ;
    skos:definition "A breakpoint is a mark on a line of code that makes the debugger pause whenever the program reaches that line. The chapter says that setting a breakpoint on a specific line forces the debugger to pause whenever the program execution reaches that line (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Breakpoint> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Breakpoint" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Controls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Breakpoint .
pg:ContinueControl a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Continue control"@en ;
    skos:definition "Continue is the control that lets a paused program run at normal speed again. The chapter says that clicking Continue causes the program to execute normally until it terminates or reaches a breakpoint, and that someone who is done debugging and wants the program to go on normally should click it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ContinueControl> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ContinueControl" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Controls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ContinueControl .
pg:StepIn a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Step in"@en ;
    skos:definition "Step In is the control that executes the next line and then pauses again. The chapter adds that if the next line is a function call, the debugger steps into that function, jumping to the function's first line of code (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_StepIn> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StepIn" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Controls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StepIn .
pg:StepOver a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Step over"@en ;
    skos:definition "Step Over is the control that executes the next line of code like Step In does, but treats a function call as a single step. The chapter says that, if the next line calls a function, Step Over steps over, or rushes through, the code in the function: its code runs at full speed and the debugger pauses as soon as the call returns (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_StepOver> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StepOver" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Controls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StepOver .
pg:StepOut a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Step out"@en ;
    skos:definition "Step Out is the control that runs the rest of the current function at full speed. The chapter says that it causes the debugger to execute lines of code at full speed until it returns from the current function, and that it is meant for the reader who has stepped into a function call with Step In and now simply wants to keep executing instructions until leaving it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_StepOut> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StepOut" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Controls .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StepOut .
pg:PythonDebugger a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Python debugger"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Python debugger is the debugger that comes with Python itself, the module pdb. The documentation says that pdb defines an interactive source code debugger for Python programs, with breakpoints, single stepping at the level of source lines, source code listing, and evaluation of Python code in the context of the paused program; it also supports post-mortem debugging, which means examining a program after it has crashed, and can be called under program control (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_PythonDebugger> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PythonDebugger" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Debugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PythonDebugger .
pg:Process a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Process"@en ;
    skos:definition "A process is a program that is running. PEP 768 speaks of running Python processes and of attaching to a live process by process ID (Galindo Salgado, Wozniski and Stojanovic, 2024)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_Process> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Process" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PythonDebugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Process .
pg:EnvironmentVariable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Environment variable"@en ;
    skos:definition "An environment variable is a named piece of text that belongs to the environment in which a process runs. It belongs to the process environment, which the program can read."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_EnvironmentVariable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-EnvironmentVariable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PythonDebugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:EnvironmentVariable .
pg:BreakpointFunction a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Breakpoint function"@en ;
    skos:definition "The built-in function breakpoint() puts a breakpoint into the program itself. Writing breakpoint() on a line makes the program stop in the debugger when it reaches that line."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_BreakpointFunction> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BreakpointFunction" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PythonDebugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BreakpointFunction .
pg:BreakpointHook a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Breakpoint hook"@en ;
    skos:definition "The breakpoint hook is the function that breakpoint() calls: sys.breakpointhook. A hook is a name that holds a function which other code calls, so replacing the function changes what happens."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_BreakpointHook> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BreakpointHook" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PythonDebugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BreakpointHook .
pg:PdbCommands a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pdb commands"@en ;
    skos:definition "The commands of pdb are the text form of the controls of the chapter. At the prompt (Pdb) the programmer types a command and presses Enter."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_PdbCommands> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PdbCommands" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PythonDebugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PdbCommands .
pg:RemoteAttach a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Remote attach"@en ;
    skos:definition "Remote attach is the ability of the debugger to join a program that is already running. Since Python 3.14 the command python -m pdb -p 1234 attaches to the process with that PID (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_RemoteAttach> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RemoteAttach" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PythonDebugger .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RemoteAttach .
pg:ModernPractice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Modern practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "Modern practice is the part of the chapter in which the four instruments, exceptions, assertions, logging and the debugger, are used together on programs that contain real mistakes. The chapter does this three times."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_ModernPractice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ModernPractice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ModernPractice .
pg:WorkedCases a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Worked cases"@en ;
    skos:definition "The worked cases are six small mistakes, each taken from one of the programs of the chapter. A case consists of the faulty line, the symptom that the program shows, the instrument that exposes the cause, and the repair."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_WorkedCases> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WorkedCases" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ModernPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WorkedCases .
pg:OffByOneRange a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Off-by-one range"@en ;
    skos:definition "An off-by-one error is a loop that runs one step too many, one too few, or from the wrong first number. The chapter's example is the factorial function, which contains the line for i in range(n + 1)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_OffByOneRange> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OffByOneRange" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkedCases .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OffByOneRange .
pg:AccumulatorStart a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Accumulator starting value"@en ;
    skos:definition "The starting value of an accumulator is the value it holds before the loop begins. An accumulator, as Chapter 3 introduced it, is a variable that collects a result over the passes of a loop: it is given a starting value, and each pass updates it with the item of that pass."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_AccumulatorStart> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AccumulatorStart" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkedCases .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AccumulatorStart .
pg:StringConcatenation a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "String concatenation"@en ;
    skos:definition "String concatenation is the joining of strings, written with the plus sign. The Python documentation lists the operation s + t as the concatenation of s and t (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_StringConcatenation> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StringConcatenation" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkedCases .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StringConcatenation .
pg:RandomInteger a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Random integer"@en ;
    skos:definition "A random integer is a whole number chosen by chance from a range. The function random.randint(a, b) returns a random integer N such that a <= N <= b, and the documentation adds that it is an alias, another name, for randrange(a, b+1) (Python Software Foundation, 2026), where randrange(start, stop) returns a randomly selected element from range(start, stop) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_RandomInteger> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RandomInteger" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkedCases .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RandomInteger .
pg:MembershipTest a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Membership test"@en ;
    skos:definition "A membership test asks whether a value is among the members of a collection. The operators in and not in do it: the language reference says x in s evaluates to True if x is a member of s and False otherwise, and x not in s is the negation (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_MembershipTest> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MembershipTest" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkedCases .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MembershipTest .
pg:TypeMismatchComparison a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Type mismatch comparison"@en ;
    skos:definition "A type mismatch comparison compares two values of different kinds, such as a number and a string. The documentation of the built-in types says that unless stated otherwise, objects of different types never compare equal (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch05#S_TypeMismatchComparison> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TypeMismatchComparison" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkedCases .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TypeMismatchComparison .
pg:BugNature sen0414:hasPanel pg:Bug, pg:LogicError, pg:Debugging .
pg:InstrumentSelection sen0414:hasPanel pg:InstrumentChoice .
pg:WorkingVocabulary sen0414:hasPanel pg:ObjectVocabulary, pg:CommandLine, pg:FileAndDirectory .
pg:Raising sen0414:hasPanel pg:RaiseStatement, pg:ExceptionClass, pg:ExceptionMessage, pg:ArgumentValidation, pg:UnhandledException .
pg:Tracebacks sen0414:hasPanel pg:ExceptionUnwinding, pg:TracebackReading, pg:FineGrainedLocations, pg:ExceptionNotes, pg:StandardErrorStream .
pg:Assert sen0414:hasPanel pg:SanityCheck, pg:AssertStatement, pg:SortedOrderCheck, pg:AssertionFailure, pg:AssertMessage, pg:FailFast, pg:AssertVersusRaise, pg:OptimisedMode, pg:CommandLineOption .
pg:Setup sen0414:hasPanel pg:BasicConfig, pg:LogFormat, pg:PercentPlaceholder, pg:LogToFile, pg:ForceReconfigure .
pg:LoggerObjects sen0414:hasPanel pg:Logger, pg:Handler, pg:LogRecord .
pg:Levels sen0414:hasPanel pg:LoggingLevels, pg:LevelThreshold, pg:DefaultLevel, pg:DisablingLogging .
pg:Practice sen0414:hasPanel pg:LoggingVersusPrint, pg:LazyFormatting .
pg:DebuggerInterface sen0414:hasPanel pg:MuEditor, pg:RunUnderDebugger, pg:ProgramState, pg:InspectorPane, pg:StopControl .
pg:Controls sen0414:hasPanel pg:Breakpoint, pg:ContinueControl, pg:StepIn, pg:StepOver, pg:StepOut .
pg:PythonDebugger sen0414:hasPanel pg:Process, pg:EnvironmentVariable, pg:BreakpointFunction, pg:BreakpointHook, pg:PdbCommands, pg:RemoteAttach .
pg:WorkedCases sen0414:hasPanel pg:OffByOneRange, pg:AccumulatorStart, pg:StringConcatenation, pg:RandomInteger, pg:MembershipTest, pg:TypeMismatchComparison .
pg:CE_01 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "box_print raises exceptions"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 1 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 7 of the deck and runs 11 lines of Python that print 4 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 7 ; sen0414:orderInTab 1 ;
    sen0414:code "def box_print(symbol, width, height):\n    if len(symbol) != 1:\n        raise Exception('Symbol must be a single character string.')\n    if width <= 2:\n        raise Exception('Width must be greater than 2.')\n    if height <= 2:\n        raise Exception('Height must be greater than 2.')\n    row = symbol + ' ' * (width - 2) + symbol\n    rows = [symbol * width] + [row] * (height - 2)\n    print('\\n'.join(rows + [symbol * width]))\nbox_print('*', 5, 4)" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "*****\n*   *\n*   *\n*****\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_01 .
pg:CE_02 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "The exception object and its message"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 2 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 8 of the deck and runs 8 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 8 ; sen0414:orderInTab 2 ;
    sen0414:code "try:\n    raise Exception('custom message')\nexcept Exception as err:\n    print(type(err).__name__, err.args, str(err))\ntry:\n    raise ValueError('a more specific class')\nexcept Exception as err:\n    print(type(err).__name__, isinstance(err, Exception))" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "Exception ('custom message',) custom message\nValueError True\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_02 .
pg:CE_03 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "An exception climbs the call stack"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 3 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 9 of the deck and runs 11 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 9 ; sen0414:orderInTab 3 ;
    sen0414:code "import traceback\ndef read_age(text):\n    return int(text)\ndef load(record):\n    return read_age(record['age'])\ntry:\n    load({'age': 'abc'})\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print('caught:', e)\n    frames = traceback.extract_tb(e.__traceback__)\n    print([f.name for f in frames])" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "caught: invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'abc'\n['<module>', 'load', 'read_age']\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_03 .
pg:CE_04 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "Notes added to an exception"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 4 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 10 of the deck and runs 10 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 10 ; sen0414:orderInTab 4 ;
    sen0414:code "import traceback\ntry:\n    try:\n        int('x')\n    except ValueError as e:\n        e.add_note('while reading the age field')\n        raise\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e.__notes__)\n    print(traceback.format_exception(e)[-1].strip())" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "['while reading the age field']\nwhile reading the age field\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_04 .
pg:CE_05 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "An assert catches a bug"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 5 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 13 of the deck and runs 9 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 13 ; sen0414:orderInTab 5 ;
    sen0414:code "ages = [26, 57, 92, 54, 22, 15, 17, 80, 47, 73]\nages.sort()\nassert ages[0] <= ages[-1]\nprint('sorted: ok')\nages.reverse()\ntry:\n    assert ages[0] <= ages[-1]\nexcept AssertionError as e:\n    print(type(e).__name__, repr(str(e)))" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "sorted: ok\nAssertionError ''\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_05 .
pg:CE_06 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "assert with a message, raise for users"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 6 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 14 of the deck and runs 11 lines of Python that print 3 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 14 ; sen0414:orderInTab 6 ;
    sen0414:code "def average(scores):\n    if len(scores) == 0:\n        raise ValueError('give at least one score')\n    total = sum(scores)\n    assert total >= 0, f'negative total: {total}'\n    return total / len(scores)\nfor data in ([4, 6], [], [-5, 1]):\n    try:\n        print(average(data))\n    except (ValueError, AssertionError) as e:\n        print(type(e).__name__ + ':', e)" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "5.0\nValueError: give at least one score\nAssertionError: negative total: -4\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_06 .
pg:CE_07 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "assert disappears under -O"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 7 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 15 of the deck and runs 7 lines of Python that print 3 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 15 ; sen0414:orderInTab 7 ;
    sen0414:code "src = 'assert False, 'boom'\\nprint('reached')'\ntry:\n    exec(compile(src, 'x', 'exec'))\nexcept AssertionError as e:\n    print('normal run:', e)\nexec(compile(src, 'x', 'exec', optimize=1))\nprint(__debug__)" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "normal run: boom\nreached\nTrue\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_07 .
pg:CE_08 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "The five logging levels"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 8 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 20 of the deck and runs 4 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 20 ; sen0414:orderInTab 8 ;
    sen0414:code "import logging\nlevels = ('DEBUG', 'INFO', 'WARNING', 'ERROR', 'CRITICAL')\nprint([(name, getattr(logging, name)) for name in levels])\nprint(logging.WARNING > logging.INFO, logging.getLevelName(30))" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "[('DEBUG', 10), ('INFO', 20), ('WARNING', 30), ('ERROR', 40), ('CRITICAL', 50)]\nTrue WARNING\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_08 .
pg:CE_09 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "basicConfig sets the threshold"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 9 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 21 of the deck and runs 9 lines of Python that print 4 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 21 ; sen0414:orderInTab 9 ;
    sen0414:code "import logging, sys\nfmt = '%(levelname)s - %(message)s'\ncfg = dict(stream=sys.stdout, format=fmt, force=True)\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, **cfg)\nlogging.debug('debug message')\nlogging.info('info message')\nlogging.warning('warning message')\nlogging.error('error message')\nlogging.critical('critical message')" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "INFO - info message\nWARNING - warning message\nERROR - error message\nCRITICAL - critical message\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_09 .
pg:CE_10 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "The format string of a log line"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 10 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 22 of the deck and runs 8 lines of Python that print 1 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 22 ; sen0414:orderInTab 10 ;
    sen0414:code "import logging, time\ntext = '%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s'\nfmt = logging.Formatter(text, datefmt='%Y-%m-%d %H:%M')\nfmt.converter = time.gmtime\nargs = ('demo', logging.WARNING, 'app.py', 7)\nrec = logging.LogRecord(*args, 'disk is %s%% full', (91,), None)\nrec.created = 1790000000\nprint(fmt.format(rec))" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "2026-09-21 14:13 - WARNING - disk is 91% full\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_10 .
pg:CE_11 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "Logging to a file"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 11 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 23 of the deck and runs 8 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 23 ; sen0414:orderInTab 11 ;
    sen0414:code "import logging, os, tempfile\npath = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(), 'myProgramLog.txt')\nfmt = '%(levelname)s - %(message)s'\ncfg = dict(format=fmt, force=True)\nlogging.basicConfig(filename=path, level=logging.DEBUG, **cfg)\nlogging.debug('to the file')\nlogging.critical('also to the file')\nprint(open(path).read(), end='')" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "DEBUG - to the file\nCRITICAL - also to the file\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_11 .
pg:CE_12 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "Switching logging off"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 12 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 24 of the deck and runs 10 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 24 ; sen0414:orderInTab 12 ;
    sen0414:code "import logging, sys\nfmt = '%(levelname)s - %(message)s'\ncfg = dict(stream=sys.stdout, format=fmt, force=True)\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, **cfg)\nlogging.critical('before disable')\nlogging.disable(logging.CRITICAL)\nlogging.critical('after disable')\nlogging.error('after disable')\nlogging.disable(logging.NOTSET)\nlogging.error('after re-enabling')" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "CRITICAL - before disable\nERROR - after re-enabling\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_12 .
pg:CE_13 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "Lazy formatting of log messages"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 13 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 25 of the deck and runs 13 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 25 ; sen0414:orderInTab 13 ;
    sen0414:code "import logging\nclass Loud:\n    calls = 0\n    def __str__(self):\n        Loud.calls += 1\n        return 'loud'\nlog = logging.getLogger('demo')\nlog.setLevel(logging.INFO)\nlog.addHandler(logging.NullHandler())\nlog.debug('value is %s', Loud())\nprint('lazy:', Loud.calls)\nlog.debug(f'value is {Loud()}')\nprint('f-string:', Loud.calls)" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "lazy: 0\nf-string: 1\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_13 .
pg:CE_14 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "Factorial with the off-by-one bug"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 14 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 28 of the deck and runs 13 lines of Python that print 7 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 28 ; sen0414:orderInTab 14 ;
    sen0414:code "import logging, sys\nfmt = '%(levelname)s - %(message)s'\ncfg = dict(stream=sys.stdout, format=fmt, force=True)\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG, **cfg)\ndef factorial(n):\n    logging.debug('Start of factorial(%s)', n)\n    total = 1\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        total *= i\n        logging.debug('i is %s, total is %s', i, total)\n    logging.debug('End of factorial(%s)', n)\n    return total\nprint(factorial(3))" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "DEBUG - Start of factorial(3)\nDEBUG - i is 0, total is 0\nDEBUG - i is 1, total is 0\nDEBUG - i is 2, total is 0\nDEBUG - i is 3, total is 0\nDEBUG - End of factorial(3)\n0\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_14 .
pg:CE_15 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "Factorial fixed, the log as proof"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 15 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 29 of the deck and runs 13 lines of Python that print 6 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 29 ; sen0414:orderInTab 15 ;
    sen0414:code "import logging, sys\nfmt = '%(levelname)s - %(message)s'\ncfg = dict(stream=sys.stdout, format=fmt, force=True)\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG, **cfg)\ndef factorial(n):\n    logging.debug('Start of factorial(%s)', n)\n    total = 1\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        total *= i\n        logging.debug('i is %s, total is %s', i, total)\n    logging.debug('End of factorial(%s)', n)\n    return total\nprint(factorial(3))" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "DEBUG - Start of factorial(3)\nDEBUG - i is 1, total is 1\nDEBUG - i is 2, total is 2\nDEBUG - i is 3, total is 6\nDEBUG - End of factorial(3)\n6\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_15 .
pg:CE_16 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "The adding program joins strings"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 16 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 30 of the deck and runs 3 lines of Python that print 2 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 30 ; sen0414:orderInTab 16 ;
    sen0414:code "first, second, third = '5', '3', '42'\nprint('The sum is ' + first + second + third)\nprint('The sum is', int(first) + int(second) + int(third))" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "The sum is 5342\nThe sum is 50\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_16 .
pg:CE_17 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "The coin toss that never wins"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 17 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 31 of the deck and runs 8 lines of Python that print 5 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 31 ; sen0414:orderInTab 17 ;
    sen0414:code "import random\nrandom.seed(7)\nguess = 'heads'    # input() always gives a str\ntoss = random.randint(0, 1)    # 0 is tails, 1 is heads\nprint(type(toss).__name__, type(guess).__name__, toss == guess)\nprint('You got it!' if toss == guess else 'Nope! Guess again!')\nfor g in ('heads', 'tails', 'maybe'):\n    print(g, 'not in tuple:', g not in ('heads', 'tails'))" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "int str False\nNope! Guess again!\nheads not in tuple: False\ntails not in tuple: False\nmaybe not in tuple: True\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_17 .
pg:CE_18 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "The coin toss, fixed"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 18 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 32 of the deck and runs 9 lines of Python that print 1 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 32 ; sen0414:orderInTab 18 ;
    sen0414:code "import random\nrandom.seed(7)\nguess = 'heads'\nbuggy = fixed = 0\nfor _ in range(1000):\n    toss = random.randint(0, 1)\n    buggy += toss == guess\n    fixed += ('heads' if toss == 1 else 'tails') == guess\nprint('buggy wins:', buggy, 'fixed wins:', fixed)" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "buggy wins: 0 fixed wins: 489\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_18 .
pg:CE_19 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "A debugger is a program that watches lines"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 19 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 37 of the deck and runs 16 lines of Python that print 6 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 37 ; sen0414:orderInTab 19 ;
    sen0414:code "import sys\ndef add(a, b):\n    total = a + b\n    return total\ndef main():\n    x = add(2, 3)\n    return add(x, 10)\nsteps = []\ndef watch(f, event, arg):\n    if event == 'line':\n        steps.append((f.f_code.co_name, dict(f.f_locals)))\n    return watch\nsys.settrace(watch)\nmain()\nsys.settrace(None)\nprint(*steps, sep='\\n')" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "('main', {})\n('add', {'a': 2, 'b': 3})\n('add', {'a': 2, 'b': 3, 'total': 5})\n('main', {'x': 5})\n('add', {'a': 5, 'b': 10})\n('add', {'a': 5, 'b': 10, 'total': 15})\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_19 .
pg:CE_20 a ipo:CodeExample ; rdfs:label "breakpoint() and the pdb commands"@en ;
    skos:definition "Example 20 of the chapter-code tab comes from slide 38 of the deck and runs 7 lines of Python that print 3 lines of output."@en ; dcterms:source "03-materials/ch05/page/chapter_code_ch05_v1_0_0.json" ;
    sen0414:slideNumber 38 ; sen0414:orderInTab 20 ;
    sen0414:code "import pdb, sys\nsys.breakpointhook = lambda: print('the hook was called')\nbreakpoint()\nsys.breakpointhook = sys.__breakpointhook__\nprint(sys.breakpointhook.__name__)\nnames = 'step next return continue break where'.split()\nprint([n for n in names if hasattr(pdb.Pdb, 'do_' + n)])" ; sen0414:expectedOutput "the hook was called\nbreakpointhook\n['step', 'next', 'return', 'continue', 'break', 'where']\n" .
pg:Tabs_learn sen0414:hasExample pg:CE_20 .
